WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.208 --> 00:00:03.586
Hi, I'm Theodore Kim.
I'm associate professor

00:00:03.586 --> 00:00:05.714
of computer science
here at Yale University,

00:00:06.297 --> 00:00:09.676
and I co-lead the Yale
Computer Graphics Lab,

00:00:09.676 --> 00:00:11.052
which we are in right now.

00:00:11.052 --> 00:00:14.347
I investigate physics
based animation problems

00:00:14.347 --> 00:00:18.435
for film and for video games
and for VR environments.

00:00:19.185 --> 00:00:22.147
One thing we've been interested
in lately is human hair.

00:00:22.522 --> 00:00:24.274
You can see it's
just a bunch of strands.

00:00:24.274 --> 00:00:27.986 size:78% position:39% line:0%
If you really look deeply
at the math for computing

00:00:27.986 --> 00:00:29.529 align:start position:0% line:0%
human hair,

00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:32.490 align:start position:0% line:0%
it's actually really formulated
for very straight hair.

00:00:32.699 --> 00:00:33.366
So this is here.

00:00:33.366 --> 00:00:34.951
You've probably seen shampoo
commercials

00:00:34.951 --> 00:00:37.704
where at the very end somebody
does a toss of their head

00:00:37.704 --> 00:00:41.458
and there's a great wave of
hair that flicks back.

00:00:41.708 --> 00:00:43.793
So most hair
algorithms have been developed

00:00:43.793 --> 00:00:46.629
for this kind of hair, but
there's lots of kinds of hair.

00:00:46.629 --> 00:00:49.340
There's very curly hair
and afro-textured hair.

00:00:49.507 --> 00:00:51.551
This actually
has not been examined

00:00:52.302 --> 00:00:54.846
almost at all in computer
graphics.

00:00:55.013 --> 00:00:58.725
So right now we are developing
algorithms for realistically

00:00:59.142 --> 00:01:02.812
simulating the motion of
extremely tightly curled hair.

00:01:03.521 --> 00:01:05.356
One of the first things
that we do is

00:01:05.356 --> 00:01:06.107
we try to look at

00:01:06.107 --> 00:01:09.527
lots of references
and then we dig into the math

00:01:09.527 --> 00:01:10.195
[solving problem]

00:01:10.195 --> 00:01:13.490
and we say, “What equations can
we come up with that

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:16.910
will efficiently capture these
important visual features?”

00:01:17.368 --> 00:01:20.079
Many of you probably have seen
the Pixar movie Soul,

00:01:20.079 --> 00:01:20.872
[trombone plays]

00:01:20.872 --> 00:01:23.583
which actually has lots of
afro-textured and kinky hair

00:01:23.583 --> 00:01:26.503
in it, simulating this hair
even for the people at Pixar,

00:01:26.586 --> 00:01:28.463
it was actually still
quite difficult

00:01:28.463 --> 00:01:29.380
because these tools,

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:31.925
they were not really made
for this type of hair.

00:01:32.467 --> 00:01:35.887
Hundreds of researchers
over decades around the world

00:01:36.221 --> 00:01:38.014
have poured lots
and lots of effort

00:01:38.014 --> 00:01:40.266
into understanding precisely

00:01:40.475 --> 00:01:43.978
what sort of math goes into this
sort of realistic depiction,

00:01:43.978 --> 00:01:47.440
and this equivalent effort
has just not been invested

00:01:47.649 --> 00:01:49.943
into afro-textured
and kinky hair yet.

00:01:50.109 --> 00:01:51.736
So our hope is that

00:01:51.736 --> 00:01:54.489
if we develop new math, new
physics, and new algorithms,

00:01:54.739 --> 00:01:58.159
then maybe next time around
it'll be much easier to make.

00:01:58.326 --> 00:01:59.410
[discussing problem]

00:01:59.410 --> 00:02:01.955
What I really look
forward to is in the future

00:02:02.288 --> 00:02:06.125
when I watch movies,
I don't feel, “Oh, I see

00:02:06.125 --> 00:02:07.252
they had to make a story

00:02:07.252 --> 00:02:11.130
decision based on the technology
of straight hair.”

00:02:11.256 --> 00:02:13.716
They could make any story choice
that they wanted to,

00:02:13.883 --> 00:02:17.053
and there is just
a huge diversity of hair.

